course: dictionary
lesson: 9089
title: A word from an old book
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words come from old books and still live on in stories and jokes. «Sluggard» is one of them. You may not know it, but the clues around it help. Look for what the sentence is about: if people are working and someone is lying in bed, the lying one is a sluggard.
explain: The Gryphon in «Alice in Wonderland» orders her to stand up and recite «'Tis the voice of the sluggard». The poem is about a lazy person who does not want to get up, so a sluggard must be a lazy person. Read the words around the hard word first, then check your meaning by putting it in the sentence.
explain: In the Bible's Book of Proverbs, the writer says, «Go to the ant, you sluggard; consider its ways and be wise.» The ant works hard and prepares, so the writer is telling the sluggard to be more like the ant. Today people sometimes use sluggard as a joke for someone who sleeps late or moves slowly.
fact: A sluggard is a very lazy person who does not want to work or get up.
fact: «Sluggard» is a noun. You can say «a sluggard» or «sluggards».
fact: The word is old and comes from the Bible and old poems. It is often used in old books.
fact: In jokes, «sluggard» means someone who sleeps late or moves slowly, not someone who is truly lazy.
fact: «Go to the ant, thou sluggard» is a phrase from the Bible that means: copy the hard-working ant.
exercise: What is a sluggard? | A busy ant that works all day long | *A person who likes to read books | A very lazy person who does not want to work or get up | A fast runner who wins every race
exercise: In the Bible, why does the writer say «Go to the ant, you sluggard»? | The sluggard is a wise king | The ant works hard and prepares | The ant is lazy and sleeps all day | *The ant is a kind friend to the sluggard
exercise: Find and copy the word in «Stand up and repeat "'Tis the voice of the sluggard"» that means a lazy person. | stand | repeat | voice | *sluggard
test: Mum laughs and says, «Morning, sluggard, it's nearly lunchtime!» What does sluggard mean in this joke? | A tiny insect that lives under a stone | A cheeky, playful way of saying someone is sleeping late | *A friend who always wakes up early and works hard | A famous poet who writes about the ant
test: Which situation best matches the word sluggard? | *A girl who finishes her homework early | A child who stays in bed while everyone else is working | A dog who runs round the park | A baker who gets up at four in the morning
exam: Which sentence uses sluggard in the humorous way? | "The sluggard wrote a poem about the sea." | "Go to the sluggard and ask for help with the bread." | "The sluggard is a kind of bird that sings at night." | *"Good morning, sluggard, it's nearly lunchtime!"
